From 008a662a46b4d449a15c722b16ccfa2c005f3c59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NEMO Phase II executor <executor@nemo.local>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 13:41:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Phase II: wire the coagulation dropped-flux overflow
 guard

Makes the Rung 1 top-bin policy (c) a monitored, loud deferral instead of an
implicit one, before the Brownian kernel (Rung 1b) can stress it.

Single source of truth for the kernel: new coag_kernel(i,j) (with the 1/2
self-pair factor) is now used BOTH by dust_coagulation_set_rates (onto
reaction_rates) and by the diagnostic, so the guard automatically tracks whatever
kernel the reactions use. Rung 1b adds its Brownian branch in this one function.

dust_coagulation_flux_diag(dropped,total) returns the coagulation collision rate
the overflow skip drops (sum over overflow pairs of kernel*n_i*n_j) and the total
over all pairs. The main loop computes it each output into globals; write_current_dust
records the dimensionless fraction dropped/total as a new dust_distribution.out
column (coag_dropped_flux_frac) and emits a loud stderr warning once it exceeds
0.1%, telling the user the analytic gate is invalid and the grid is too small.

Verified:
  * valid 16-bin gate run: dropped fraction ~1e-51 -> ~8e-26 across the knee, no
    warning; gate (a)/(b1) unchanged (5.4e-15 / 2.2e-5) since the diagnostic is
    read-only on abundances.
  * deliberately-undersized 6-bin grid: fraction climbs 5e-16 -> 0.99, warning
    fires exactly as it crosses 0.1% (t~366 yr), and mass stays conserved to
    ~2e-15 throughout -- confirming the skip drops FLUX, never mass.
  * coagulation-OFF still byte-identical: jac_weight_check unchanged (13450
    entries, worst reldiff 1.8006e-3, 0 weight bugs).

Next: Brownian kernel (Rung 1b) in coag_kernel + its temperature/size dependence
recomputed per macro-step in set_dependant_rates.
